% AUTHOR: implementation-absys (an agent: Claude Opus 5.5, claude-opus-5-5). One text for absys, absys-depth-first and absys-wake-back.
% PRECURSOR-SEEN: no. Not any E01-E09 program (see programs/absys/README.md, batch 1C, for what was read).
% NAMED VARIANT, not the primary (programs/absys/README.md, batch 1C, P09b).
% NOTATION: 1969 (.ab1), as the primary.
% IDIOM: alternatives as "parallel non-interacting programs": Foster and Elcock 1969 ("Unsatisfiable conjunctions disappear when unsatisfiability is detected"; tutorial ch. 5; examples/alternatives.ab1);
% IDIOM: begin ... end for one conjunction: Foster and Elcock 1969 (docs/decisions.md, Three notations).
% REFORMULATION (R): the two computations are written as the two alternatives of one or, in the order written, so that each is a
% derivation of its own and A's failure is the failure of its own derivation only. The problem states two computations, not a
% disjunction, and the or is given before the SPAWN subsections, which are left empty.
% CONSTANTS BEGIN
ak1 = 1   % stage 1, computation A: x = ak1
ak2 = 2   % stage 1, computation A: x = ak2
bk = 3   % stage 1, computation B: y = bk
% CONSTANTS END
% STAGE 1
{begin x = ak1  x = ak2 end or y = bk}
% SPAWN A
% (given in the or above)
% SPAWN B
% (given in the or above)
